  "aic-4887": {
    "description": "Claude Monet painted *Irises* in France between 1914 and 1917, during World War I, as part of a series that enlarged familiar motifs to monumental scale. At roughly six and a half feet square, the oil painting has no clear horizon or depth: flowers and foliage seem to float across a dense, disorienting surface suggestive of water. Kept in Monet’s Giverny studio after his death, it was damaged locally by wartime shell shrapnel and rediscovered in the 1950s. The Art Institute of Chicago acquired it in 1956; curator Katherine Kuh saw an affinity between Monet’s late experiments and contemporary large-scale abstraction.",
    "descriptionTranslations": {
      "ja": "クロード・モネは第一次世界大戦中の1914〜17年、親しみのあるモチーフを大画面に展開する連作の一作として、フランスで《アイリス》を描いた。約2メートル四方の油彩画には明確な地平線や奥行きがなく、水面を思わせる密度の高い画面に、花や葉が浮かぶように配されている。モネの死後もジヴェルニーのアトリエに残され、第二次世界大戦中の砲弾の破片による局所的な損傷を受けた後、1950年代に再発見された。1956年にシカゴ美術館が購入し、学芸員キャサリン・クーは、モネ晩年の実験的な絵画と当時の大画面抽象絵画との形式的な親近性に着目した。"
    },
    "descriptionKind": "museum_text",
    "descriptionVersion": "openai-bilingual-v1",
    "descriptionAttribution": "Art Institute of Chicago",
    "descriptionSourceUrl": "https://www.artic.edu/artworks/4887",
    "descriptionLicense": "CC BY 4.0",
    "descriptionLicenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/",
    "descriptionModifications": "Museum text summarized and translated into Japanese using OpenAI (gpt-6-luna; openai-bilingual-v1)."
  },
  "aic-81539": {
    "description": "Claude Monet’s future wife, Camille Doncieux, sits on an island in the Seine, facing the hamlet of Gloton beside Bennecourt. She and Monet are thought to have rowed there from the village. The painting is the sole surviving work from the couple’s brief stay at Gloton, a rural retreat near Paris suggested to Monet by the novelist Émile Zola. Traces of revisions beneath the paint indicate that Camille may originally have been shown holding a bonneted child, presumed to be their baby, Jean.",
    "descriptionTranslations": {
      "ja": "セーヌ川の中州に座るカミーユ・ドンシューは、ベヌクール近くの集落グロトンを見つめています。彼女はモネの将来の妻で、二人は村から舟でここへ来たと考えられています。小説家エミール・ゾラがモネに勧めた、パリから行きやすいグロトンでの短い滞在を伝える、現存唯一の作品です。絵具の下に残る描き直しの痕跡から、当初のカミーユは、二人の子ジャンと思われる帽子をかぶった幼児を抱いていた可能性が示されています。"
    },
    "descriptionKind": "museum_text",
    "descriptionVersion": "openai-bilingual-v1",
    "descriptionAttribution": "Art Institute of Chicago",
    "descriptionSourceUrl": "https://www.artic.edu/artworks/81539",
    "descriptionLicense": "CC BY 4.0",
    "descriptionLicenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/",
    "descriptionModifications": "Museum text summarized and translated into Japanese using OpenAI (gpt-6-luna; openai-bilingual-v1)."
  },
  "aic-111295": {
    "description": "On March 9, 1819, Philadelphia’s Masonic Hall, built only eight years earlier, burned to the ground. Publisher-commissioned as the basis for a news print etched by John Hill, this oil on mahogany panel is Samuel Jones’s study of the fire’s devastation. John Lewis Krimmel was hired to develop the foreground figures for the print, but whether he contributed to the figures in this painting is unknown. Such depictions helped circulate news of sensational events and brought fine art to a wider audience.",
    "descriptionTranslations": {
      "ja": "1819年3月9日、フィラデルフィアのメイソニック・ホールが焼失しました。建設からわずか8年後のことです。木版ではなくジョン・ヒルがエッチングしたニュース版画のために出版社が依頼した本作は、サミュエル・ジョーンズによる火災の習作で、マホガニー板に油彩で描かれています。版画ではジョン・ルイス・クリンメルが前景の人物表現を練り直しましたが、本作の人物にも関与したかは定かではありません。こうした作品は、衝撃的な事件を伝え、美術をより広い人々に届ける役割を担いました。"
    },
    "descriptionKind": "museum_text",
    "descriptionVersion": "openai-bilingual-v1",
    "descriptionAttribution": "Art Institute of Chicago",
    "descriptionSourceUrl": "https://www.artic.edu/artworks/111295",
    "descriptionLicense": "CC BY 4.0",
    "descriptionLicenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/",
    "descriptionModifications": "Museum text summarized and translated into Japanese using OpenAI (gpt-6-luna; openai-bilingual-v1)."
  },
  "aic-9021": {
    "description": "Among the German Expressionists, artists who used strong color and exaggerated form to express emotional content, Franz Marc was especially committed to his empathic interest in the life of animals. \"Is there a more mysterious idea,\" he asked, \"than to imagine how nature is reflected in the eyes of animals?\" Beginning in 1905, he devoted himself to representing the world with a fresh and purifying vision. Marc painted The Bewitched Mill following a sojourn to the Italian town of Merano in the southern Tirol. The work's title refers to the \"magical\" harmony he sensed there between human life, represented by the houses and mill on the left, and nature, embodied by the lyrical region of trees and animals on the right.\n\nCatalogue information\nRecorded artist or maker: Franz Marc German, 1880–1916. Date: 1913. Materials and technique: Oil on canvas. Place of creation: Germany. Classification: oil on canvas. Physical dimensions: Without frame: 130.2 × 90.8 cm (51 5/16 × 35 3/4 in.); 130.2 × 91.2 cm (51 1/4 × 35 7/8 in.). Museum catalogue subjects: Century of Progress, world's fairs, Chicago World's Fairs, deers, animals, birds, trees. Museum credit line: Arthur Jerome Eddy Memorial Collection. Recorded ownership history (excerpt): The artist, Munich; sold to Arthur Jerome Eddy (1859–1920), Chicago, 1914 [Barnett 2022]; by descent to his wife, Lucy O. Eddy (1863-1931) and son, Jerome O. Eddy (1891-1951), Chicago, 1920; given to the Art Institute of Chicago, 1931.. Exhibition record (excerpt): Chicago, Art Institute, Exhibition of Paintings from the Collection of the Late Arthur Jerome Eddy, September 19-October 22, 1922, cat. 50. Chicago, Art Institute, The Arthur Jerome Eddy Collection of Modern Paintings and Sculpture, December 22, 1931-January 17, 1932, cat. 12….",
    "descriptionKind": "museum_text",
    "descriptionSourceUrl": "https://www.artic.edu/artworks/9021",
    "descriptionAttribution": "Art Institute of Chicago",
    "descriptionLicense": "CC BY 4.0",
    "descriptionLicenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/",
    "descriptionModifications": "Museum text converted to plain text; catalogue facts added; abridged if necessary."
  }
